  "script": "Players who could've had a better career, but SACRIFICED for the team! In 2002, Arsenal signed a Brazilian midfielder named Gilberto Silva, who just started the World Cup final for Brazil and only cost 4.5 million pounds. Gilberto was the kind of player who could score from anywhere, holding the record for Arsenal's fastest ever goal at 20 seconds against PSV in the Champions League, while also scoring the first ever Premier League goal at the Emirates Stadium. But what Wenger asked him to do with all that talent is the frustrating part. Instead of playing as a box-to-box midfielder, Gilberto was told to do the one job nobody wants, sit deep and shield the back four so everyone else could attack, and he said yes. He became the anchor that allowed Vieira to charge forward and gave Henry the freedom to do whatever he wanted. With a Brazilian magazine describing him as the man who carries the piano so others play their tunes on it. During Arsenal's Invincible season, where they went an entire league campaign unbeaten, Gilberto played in 32 of those 38 matches. But here's the part that says everything about what he gave up. They nicknamed him 'The Invisible Wall' because the better he played, the less anyone noticed him. And despite winning a Premier League title and two FA Cups on top of his World Cup medal, to this day, he's still considered one of the most underrated players in Arsenal's history, all because he chose to make everyone around him look better than himself.",
